WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.510 --> 00:00:13.150
- Hello, welcome to this
Monday's daily homeroom.

00:00:13.150 --> 00:00:15.110
For those of you all who are new here,

00:00:15.110 --> 00:00:17.060
this is something we're trying to do

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:18.410
to keep us all connected

00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:20.576
as we have these school closures going on

00:00:20.576 --> 00:00:22.450
throughout the world.

00:00:22.450 --> 00:00:23.840
Many of y'all know Khan Academy,

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:24.890
we're a not for profit

00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:25.890
with a mission of providing

00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:27.560
a free world class education

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:29.310
for anyone anywhere.

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:30.880
And when we found out

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:32.700
that the school closures were happening,

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:34.840
we realized that we had to do many things.

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:36.350
That as a not for profit
with this mission,

00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:39.130
we had to step up to support you,

00:00:39.130 --> 00:00:42.024
students, parents, and
teachers, any way we could.

00:00:42.024 --> 00:00:44.840
So we had already, over
the last many years,

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:46.740
we could have never
foreseen this situation.

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:48.260
We had built content.

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:49.410
There's Khan Academy kids,

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:51.530
which is for the young crowd,

00:00:51.530 --> 00:00:54.250
ages two to six in reading, writing,

00:00:54.250 --> 00:00:55.340
social emotional learning.

00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:56.870
And then on Khan Academy,

00:00:56.870 --> 00:00:58.830
not Khan Academy kids,

00:00:58.830 --> 00:01:00.420
you can continue on in math,

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:02.410
English and language arts, the sciences,

00:01:02.410 --> 00:01:05.300
humanities, SAT practice.

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:07.423
And we realized that we
should also do things

00:01:07.423 --> 00:01:08.970
that can help structure your day.

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:11.410
So we released some of
those daily schedules out

00:01:11.410 --> 00:01:12.243
a couple of weeks ago

00:01:12.243 --> 00:01:15.330
that we've heard good
feedback from all of y'all.

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:17.020
We've been doing parent webinars,

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:18.190
teacher webinars,

00:01:18.190 --> 00:01:20.710
and we are doing this daily homeroom,

00:01:20.710 --> 00:01:23.500
which is a place to answer
any questions you might have.

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:26.390
So as you listen, definitely and whether

00:01:26.390 --> 00:01:28.100
it's on Facebook or YouTube,

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:30.396
type in questions for myself or our guest,

00:01:30.396 --> 00:01:32.830
who is gonna be Adam Green
from our content team.

00:01:32.830 --> 00:01:34.330
And we're going to try to answer

00:01:34.330 --> 00:01:35.460
as many as we can

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:37.677
over the course of the next few minutes.

00:01:37.677 --> 00:01:39.671
I do wanna give a special thanks

00:01:39.671 --> 00:01:42.620
to several corporations
who have stepped up

00:01:42.620 --> 00:01:43.970
in the last couple of weeks.

00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:47.802
Khan Academy is funded with
philanthropic donations.

00:01:47.802 --> 00:01:50.527
And we were running at
a deficit even before

00:01:50.527 --> 00:01:53.806
our traffic spiked by
two and a half, three X

00:01:53.806 --> 00:01:56.660
over the last couple of weeks.

00:01:56.660 --> 00:02:00.440
And Bank of America followed
by AT&amp;T, google.org,

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:02.040
and Novartis have really stepped up

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:04.150
and helped us close some of that gap.

00:02:04.150 --> 00:02:06.530
But I do wanna emphasize
any corporations listening

00:02:06.530 --> 00:02:08.090
or any individuals listening,

00:02:08.090 --> 00:02:10.060
every dollar matters.

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:11.810
We are still running at a deficit,

00:02:11.810 --> 00:02:14.960
and we want the resources
to be able to support you

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:15.837
as well as possible.

00:02:15.837 --> 00:02:18.784
And folks who are not
in a position to donate

00:02:18.784 --> 00:02:22.060
so that everyone has access to things

00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:24.180
that can bridge them
through this time period.

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:28.340
So keep putting your questions
in on YouTube and Facebook.

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:30.720
And I'd like to introduce
our guest, Adam Green,

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:31.553
who runs

00:02:31.553 --> 00:02:33.700
who's the head of our U.S. content

00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:34.540
on Khan Academy.

00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:35.550
Adam, good to see you.

00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:37.030
- Good to see you.

00:02:37.030 --> 00:02:37.863
Thanks Sal.

00:02:37.863 --> 00:02:39.840
We're also joined here by Pistachio.

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:41.810
I know some of the folks in the office,

00:02:41.810 --> 00:02:43.730
Pistachio usually comes
into the office with me

00:02:43.730 --> 00:02:45.400
and I know that they're missing her.

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:47.890
So I wanted to say hello from her.

00:02:47.890 --> 00:02:49.300
And also there's a neighborhood cat

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:51.770
that she usually likes to bark
at about this time of day.

00:02:51.770 --> 00:02:53.090
So this is the best way to make sure

00:02:53.090 --> 00:02:54.210
that she's not going to be barking

00:02:54.210 --> 00:02:55.635
during this conversation,

00:02:55.635 --> 00:02:57.180
but a great to see you.

00:02:57.180 --> 00:02:58.013
Thanks Sal.

00:02:58.990 --> 00:03:02.700
- Yes, I have, I have a mixed
feelings towards Pistachio.

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:04.212
One of the cutest dogs I know,

00:03:04.212 --> 00:03:07.130
(Adam laughing)
but I think they know that

00:03:07.130 --> 00:03:09.290
and so doesn't always
give me the attention

00:03:09.290 --> 00:03:11.006
that I think I deserve.

00:03:11.006 --> 00:03:13.800
(laughing)

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:15.920
- Treats. Treats are the way to go.

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:16.753
- Treats.

00:03:16.753 --> 00:03:17.586
Yeah, bribery.

00:03:17.586 --> 00:03:19.513
I don't normally pay for, anyway.

00:03:21.410 --> 00:03:22.610
What Adam,

00:03:22.610 --> 00:03:25.320
maybe the place to start
for everyone listening

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:27.720
is what do you do here at Khan Academy

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:30.760
and what does the content
team do here at Khan Academy?

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:31.593
- Sure, thanks.

00:03:31.593 --> 00:03:33.610
Yeah, so I lead the content team,

00:03:33.610 --> 00:03:35.420
the U.S. academic content team,

00:03:35.420 --> 00:03:36.640
which basically means I work

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:38.200
with about 15 people

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:40.740
that are all really
amazing content experts

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:44.170
in everything from math
to science to humanities,

00:03:44.170 --> 00:03:46.530
social studies, English language arts,

00:03:46.530 --> 00:03:47.670
and we all work together

00:03:47.670 --> 00:03:49.040
to build all the great content

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:52.393
that you see on Khan Academy
for those core courses.

00:03:53.273 --> 00:03:55.530
And we're constantly --
- And how does it work?

00:03:55.530 --> 00:03:56.799
- Working to add more.

00:03:56.799 --> 00:03:57.632
You know, go ahead.

00:03:57.632 --> 00:03:58.720
Go ahead Sal.

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:00.380
- Yeah, well actually
going on to adding more.

00:04:00.380 --> 00:04:02.010
One question I always get is,

00:04:02.010 --> 00:04:05.120
well, how does Khan Academy
determine what content to add?

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:07.330
And then how does it go
about adding that content?

00:04:07.330 --> 00:04:08.330
Like what's a day in the life

00:04:08.330 --> 00:04:11.140
of a content team member
here at Khan Academy?

00:04:11.140 --> 00:04:12.600
- Sure. I think that's one of the things

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:13.900
if you were to ask a content creator,

00:04:13.900 --> 00:04:16.070
that's one of the things that
they really like about the job

00:04:16.070 --> 00:04:18.080
is that there's all
sorts of different things

00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:18.913
that can be happening

00:04:18.913 --> 00:04:21.110
depending on where you are in
the life cycle of a course.

00:04:21.110 --> 00:04:23.040
So we do a lot of research to figure out

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:25.940
what really does do learners
and teachers need out there.

00:04:25.940 --> 00:04:28.234
So, everything from surveying teachers

00:04:28.234 --> 00:04:30.450
to looking at lesson
plans that are out there,

00:04:30.450 --> 00:04:32.341
to looking at state
and national standards,

00:04:32.341 --> 00:04:35.474
to see really what can we
provide on our platform

00:04:35.474 --> 00:04:36.745
that can be of highest use

00:04:36.745 --> 00:04:39.345
to learners and teachers out there.

00:04:39.345 --> 00:04:40.770
So we do that research phase

00:04:40.770 --> 00:04:44.700
and then there's a whole
series of different phases

00:04:44.700 --> 00:04:45.533
for content building

00:04:45.533 --> 00:04:48.360
depending on what type of content
we're actually working on.

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:50.713
If it's videos, articles, exercises,

00:04:50.713 --> 00:04:54.120
all of those go through a
pretty stringent review process,

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:55.846
both internally and externally

00:04:55.846 --> 00:04:57.750
as we create those resources.

00:04:57.750 --> 00:04:59.440
So we'll be building them as a team.

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:01.050
The math team will get
together and decide,

00:05:01.050 --> 00:05:02.870
hey, we need to do more geometry.

00:05:02.870 --> 00:05:04.502
We have to add geometry exercises

00:05:04.502 --> 00:05:05.840
in the next couple months.

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:06.870
That's what we've determined

00:05:06.870 --> 00:05:10.373
will be the most use to, to
teachers and learners out there.

00:05:10.373 --> 00:05:11.960
So they'll start to build that.

00:05:11.960 --> 00:05:12.930
They'll build a plan.

00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:14.803
Then they'll be building those exercises.

00:05:14.803 --> 00:05:16.507
Reviewing them internally

00:05:16.507 --> 00:05:18.468
and then sending them for external review

00:05:18.468 --> 00:05:21.830
to trusted teachers and
other folks out there,

00:05:21.830 --> 00:05:22.970
other content experts.

00:05:22.970 --> 00:05:24.500
And then we'll publish them on the site

00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:25.770
and then we're not done there.

00:05:25.770 --> 00:05:27.500
We get a lot of feedback from folks,

00:05:27.500 --> 00:05:28.800
like the folks that are watching

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:30.640
this YouTube live stream right now.

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:32.270
We looked at every single one

00:05:32.270 --> 00:05:33.440
of those pieces of feedback

00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:35.310
that we get from folks out there.

00:05:35.310 --> 00:05:37.120
And then we'll take
that into consideration

00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:39.300
when we work to improve the content

00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:40.250
that we've put out.

00:05:41.490 --> 00:05:42.890
- And what's in the queue right now?

00:05:42.890 --> 00:05:45.470
What can users out there expect to see,

00:05:45.470 --> 00:05:46.843
either improvements or new courses

00:05:46.843 --> 00:05:48.950
over the next couple of months?

00:05:48.950 --> 00:05:49.840
- Yeah, great question.

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:50.760
So we're,

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:52.590
some of those were
pushing out more quickly

00:05:52.590 --> 00:05:54.240
than we originally anticipated.

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:55.840
We had a whole bunch of stuff planned

00:05:55.840 --> 00:05:56.720
for the summer

00:05:56.720 --> 00:05:58.730
and some those things
we're releasing earlier

00:05:58.730 --> 00:06:01.080
because of this crisis
that we're in right now.

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:03.869
So you might've seen
we have new AP biology

00:06:03.869 --> 00:06:06.281
and AP chemistry content that's out there

00:06:06.281 --> 00:06:08.208
to help students with AP courses.

00:06:08.208 --> 00:06:10.545
We have all sorts of English
language arts content

00:06:10.545 --> 00:06:11.890
that's been coming out

00:06:11.890 --> 00:06:12.810
and will continue to come out

00:06:12.810 --> 00:06:14.250
over the next couple of months.

00:06:14.250 --> 00:06:16.390
And we have big refreshes on the math side

00:06:16.390 --> 00:06:19.140
in geometry and in algebra.

00:06:19.140 --> 00:06:21.314
And those are, those are
sort of the big projects

00:06:21.314 --> 00:06:23.140
that are happening right now.

00:06:23.140 --> 00:06:25.710
And in the pipeline we
have all sorts of other,

00:06:25.710 --> 00:06:27.870
other projects for
improvements in physics,

00:06:27.870 --> 00:06:29.270
AP physics one.

00:06:29.270 --> 00:06:30.630
And also we're looking to,

00:06:30.630 --> 00:06:31.588
to build a civics course

00:06:31.588 --> 00:06:34.145
that hopefully the first
part of that civics course

00:06:34.145 --> 00:06:36.070
should be out this summer.

00:06:36.070 --> 00:06:39.142
And then we have a whole
new world history project

00:06:39.142 --> 00:06:41.040
that we've developed in partnership

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:41.987
with the Gates Foundation.

00:06:41.987 --> 00:06:45.040
And that's actually hopefully
gonna be coming live

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:45.950
tomorrow on this site,

00:06:45.950 --> 00:06:47.100
which is very exciting.

00:06:49.060 --> 00:06:50.054
- Oh, it's very, yeah,

00:06:50.054 --> 00:06:51.550
all of this is very exciting stuff.

00:06:51.550 --> 00:06:53.367
So we're getting a lot of
questions about AP exams.

00:06:53.367 --> 00:06:55.376
But I do want to let
everyone know that the,

00:06:55.376 --> 00:06:58.020
the president and CEO of the CollegeBoard,

00:06:58.020 --> 00:06:59.227
the folks who make the AP

00:06:59.227 --> 00:07:00.550
and the SAT,

00:07:00.550 --> 00:07:02.570
he's going to be on this
live stream tomorrow.

00:07:02.570 --> 00:07:03.512
David Coleman.

00:07:03.512 --> 00:07:06.560
But Adam, what, how should someone

00:07:06.560 --> 00:07:08.440
who is thinking about the AP exams,

00:07:08.440 --> 00:07:09.430
I guess we're about,

00:07:09.430 --> 00:07:11.264
oh really were weeks away from it now.

00:07:11.264 --> 00:07:13.775
How would you recommend students

00:07:13.775 --> 00:07:16.830
typically prepare for
it using Khan Academy?

00:07:16.830 --> 00:07:18.850
And I know there's a lot of,

00:07:18.850 --> 00:07:20.530
you know, consternation maybe

00:07:20.530 --> 00:07:22.400
about how things might
be different this year.

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:23.670
What advice are you giving the students,

00:07:23.670 --> 00:07:26.910
especially relative to how
they could use our resources?

00:07:26.910 --> 00:07:27.990
- Yeah, there's a lot packed in there.

00:07:27.990 --> 00:07:29.044
So I'll start off by talking about

00:07:29.044 --> 00:07:31.490
how I would recommend using our resources.

00:07:31.490 --> 00:07:32.520
And I think that you have some,

00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:35.040
you've had some good advice
in the past too on that.

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:36.640
And we have a whole support page

00:07:36.640 --> 00:07:39.021
that we've dedicated to the AP

00:07:39.021 --> 00:07:40.260
be different this year

00:07:40.260 --> 00:07:42.770
and how you can use Khan
Academy resources to help you.

00:07:42.770 --> 00:07:43.603
But I think that really

00:07:43.603 --> 00:07:44.940
the first thing that I would suggest

00:07:44.940 --> 00:07:46.250
is to make an account.

00:07:46.250 --> 00:07:47.430
It's free to do that.

00:07:47.430 --> 00:07:48.600
You can use our resources

00:07:48.600 --> 00:07:49.590
without making an account,

00:07:49.590 --> 00:07:50.900
but by having that free account,

00:07:50.900 --> 00:07:52.127
you can track your progress

00:07:52.127 --> 00:07:54.230
and really use all of the tools

00:07:54.230 --> 00:07:55.530
available on Khan Academy

00:07:55.530 --> 00:07:56.720
that will help show you

00:07:56.720 --> 00:07:58.470
what you need to practice
a little bit more

00:07:58.470 --> 00:08:00.490
and what maybe you can
practice a little bit less

00:08:00.490 --> 00:08:01.840
to get prepared for the,

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:02.880
for the exam.

00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:03.955
So once you make that account,

00:08:03.955 --> 00:08:06.020
I think that there's
really one of two paths

00:08:06.020 --> 00:08:06.853
that I would recommend.

00:08:06.853 --> 00:08:08.040
If you're the kind of student

00:08:08.040 --> 00:08:09.742
where you know that there's a unit

00:08:09.742 --> 00:08:12.470
or some topic in the AP subject course

00:08:12.470 --> 00:08:14.350
that you're about to to take the exam for.

00:08:14.350 --> 00:08:16.070
If you know that there's a particular unit

00:08:16.070 --> 00:08:17.360
that you need to brush up on,

00:08:17.360 --> 00:08:19.820
then I would just go right
to that unit on Khan Academy.

00:08:19.820 --> 00:08:22.006
Let's say you're an AP statistics student

00:08:22.006 --> 00:08:23.000
and you know,

00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:24.605
probability has been
kind of getting you down,

00:08:24.605 --> 00:08:27.416
then you can go right to
the AP statistics course

00:08:27.416 --> 00:08:29.440
and jump into that unit

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:30.344
and start practicing.

00:08:30.344 --> 00:08:32.358
Watch some videos and articles.

00:08:32.358 --> 00:08:33.540
Read some articles,

00:08:33.540 --> 00:08:35.067
and then do some practice to,

00:08:35.067 --> 00:08:36.453
to get boned up on the,

00:08:36.453 --> 00:08:38.179
brushed up on those sorts of,

00:08:38.179 --> 00:08:40.115
on that sort of topic

00:08:40.115 --> 00:08:42.472
and really feel more comfortable

00:08:42.472 --> 00:08:44.620
with that particular unit.

00:08:44.620 --> 00:08:46.020
If you're not sure where you should start

00:08:46.020 --> 00:08:47.330
or you're already feeling pretty good,

00:08:47.330 --> 00:08:48.390
then my suggestion would be

00:08:48.390 --> 00:08:49.793
to jump right to the course challenge.

00:08:49.793 --> 00:08:53.220
Most of the AP courses
that Khan Academy offers

00:08:53.220 --> 00:08:54.580
have practice content.

00:08:54.580 --> 00:08:56.740
Most of those courses will also

00:08:56.740 --> 00:08:58.580
have mastery enabled features.

00:08:58.580 --> 00:08:59.841
And one of those master enabled features

00:08:59.841 --> 00:09:01.900
is the course challenge.

00:09:01.900 --> 00:09:02.970
So if you scroll all the way down

00:09:02.970 --> 00:09:04.950
to the bottom of any particular AP course,

00:09:04.950 --> 00:09:06.440
you'll see course challenge.

00:09:06.440 --> 00:09:07.620
You can click that button.

00:09:07.620 --> 00:09:11.700
It's like a 30 to 45
minute investment in time.

00:09:11.700 --> 00:09:13.010
It's 30 questions.

00:09:13.010 --> 00:09:13.979
And that really gives you

00:09:13.979 --> 00:09:17.900
a big survey of all of the
different types of questions

00:09:17.900 --> 00:09:20.070
that you're likely to
see in the entire course.

00:09:20.070 --> 00:09:20.960
Once you get through that,

00:09:20.960 --> 00:09:22.400
you'll get a bit of a report

00:09:22.400 --> 00:09:24.290
that shows you sort of
how you're doing on those

00:09:24.290 --> 00:09:25.740
and it can tell you which topics

00:09:25.740 --> 00:09:29.560
that we think that you should
focus on a little bit more.

00:09:29.560 --> 00:09:31.550
Keep in mind that the AP format

00:09:31.550 --> 00:09:32.457
is a bit different this year

00:09:32.457 --> 00:09:34.480
and one of the pieces that's different

00:09:34.480 --> 00:09:36.500
is the amount of content
that's gonna be covered

00:09:36.500 --> 00:09:37.537
on that exam.

00:09:37.537 --> 00:09:40.820
In most cases it's about 75% of the course

00:09:40.820 --> 00:09:41.820
or what most course,

00:09:41.820 --> 00:09:43.606
most classrooms will have covered

00:09:43.606 --> 00:09:45.650
by early March of this year.

00:09:45.650 --> 00:09:47.074
And these course challenges,

00:09:47.074 --> 00:09:48.790
they cover everything.

00:09:48.790 --> 00:09:50.290
So you,

00:09:50.290 --> 00:09:51.123
in my opinion,

00:09:51.123 --> 00:09:53.890
you should try to get
through all of the content

00:09:53.890 --> 00:09:54.723
for that course

00:09:54.723 --> 00:09:55.936
because that'll really prepare you for

00:09:55.936 --> 00:09:58.420
for the best experience in college

00:09:58.420 --> 00:10:01.015
if you end up applying this
credit to college credits.

00:10:01.015 --> 00:10:04.240
But the AP exam, this year itself,

00:10:04.240 --> 00:10:07.890
will only cover the first
about 75% of of the class.

00:10:07.890 --> 00:10:09.990
And you can get more information
about what that means,

00:10:09.990 --> 00:10:11.314
which units are covered and not covered,

00:10:11.314 --> 00:10:14.067
both in that AP support post

00:10:14.067 --> 00:10:15.690
that that we have on,

00:10:15.690 --> 00:10:17.420
on Khan Academy

00:10:17.420 --> 00:10:19.080
and also through the CollegeBoard website,

00:10:19.080 --> 00:10:20.515
where you can get really specific details

00:10:20.515 --> 00:10:22.763
about which units will
be covered on the exam

00:10:22.763 --> 00:10:25.560
and which ones won't
be covered on the exam.

00:10:25.560 --> 00:10:27.170
So those are sort of the two ways

00:10:27.170 --> 00:10:28.047
that I would approach it.

00:10:28.047 --> 00:10:29.250
Targeted practice.

00:10:29.250 --> 00:10:30.790
If you know you need targeted practice

00:10:30.790 --> 00:10:32.660
and if you're not quite sure
you're feeling pretty good,

00:10:32.660 --> 00:10:34.200
then jump into that course challenge

00:10:34.200 --> 00:10:36.580
and let our tools tell you

00:10:36.580 --> 00:10:38.260
where you might want to focus on more.

00:10:38.260 --> 00:10:40.620
But you may have other
suggestions too Sal.

00:10:40.620 --> 00:10:41.920
I'd love to hear from you.

00:10:42.990 --> 00:10:44.668
- Oh, well everything you just said,

00:10:44.668 --> 00:10:46.420
I think what, you know,

00:10:46.420 --> 00:10:47.300
in previous years,

00:10:47.300 --> 00:10:49.340
the standard AP format is you have,

00:10:49.340 --> 00:10:50.750
you know, roughly half multiple choice

00:10:50.750 --> 00:10:52.630
and then you have the
free response section.

00:10:52.630 --> 00:10:53.856
And I think the Khan Academy,

00:10:53.856 --> 00:10:55.330
you know, our modality,

00:10:55.330 --> 00:10:56.390
we have multiple choice

00:10:56.390 --> 00:10:58.960
and we also have things
like free response,

00:10:58.960 --> 00:11:00.550
numeric entry, not you know,

00:11:00.550 --> 00:11:01.923
long form free response.

00:11:01.923 --> 00:11:04.420
But that really prepares
you historically well

00:11:04.420 --> 00:11:06.490
for the multiple choice section of the AP.

00:11:06.490 --> 00:11:09.060
And I think to a reasonable
degree for the free response.

00:11:09.060 --> 00:11:11.260
But what I've always told students is

00:11:11.260 --> 00:11:13.670
get to as much mastery as
you can on Khan Academy

00:11:13.670 --> 00:11:15.270
Just as like this you mentioned.

00:11:15.270 --> 00:11:18.750
And then a few days or
weeks before the exam,

00:11:18.750 --> 00:11:20.560
go through the worked examples

00:11:20.560 --> 00:11:23.140
of the free response
questions on Khan Academy.

00:11:23.140 --> 00:11:24.424
These are in video format.

00:11:24.424 --> 00:11:28.030
And you know, when I or
some other content creator

00:11:28.030 --> 00:11:29.150
gives the question,

00:11:29.150 --> 00:11:30.660
pause it, try to solve it on your

00:11:30.660 --> 00:11:32.902
by yourself before we solve it.

00:11:32.902 --> 00:11:35.050
And that'll really help
you for the free response.

00:11:35.050 --> 00:11:36.540
And I think that same combination,

00:11:36.540 --> 00:11:38.010
it's going to be really
effective for this year.

00:11:38.010 --> 00:11:40.584
Where it's gonna be a shorter
free response focused,

00:11:40.584 --> 00:11:42.990
virtually proctored exam.

00:11:42.990 --> 00:11:44.830
But I think either way, as Adam mentioned,

00:11:44.830 --> 00:11:46.790
that's what's going to make you feel

00:11:46.790 --> 00:11:50.770
very comfortable and confident
in this year's exams.

00:11:50.770 --> 00:11:53.330
So we have some questions--
- Yeah so to double down.

00:11:53.330 --> 00:11:54.430
Even before you go on Sal.

00:11:54.430 --> 00:11:56.200
Just to double down on that.

00:11:56.200 --> 00:11:58.420
Not only do we have those
free response videos

00:11:58.420 --> 00:12:00.370
that students can use in
that sort of active way

00:12:00.370 --> 00:12:01.340
to really pause the video,

00:12:01.340 --> 00:12:02.560
try on their own.

00:12:02.560 --> 00:12:03.393
And then,

00:12:03.393 --> 00:12:05.300
and then watching your response to that.

00:12:05.300 --> 00:12:08.290
In the AP resources blog posts

00:12:08.290 --> 00:12:09.390
that we have on Khan Academy,

00:12:09.390 --> 00:12:11.490
we have links to free response questions

00:12:11.490 --> 00:12:12.803
from previous exams.

00:12:14.500 --> 00:12:16.420
For most exams it's the last three years,

00:12:16.420 --> 00:12:17.810
those go right to CollegeBoard

00:12:17.810 --> 00:12:20.133
and have PDFs of the
free response questions

00:12:20.133 --> 00:12:22.577
that you can practice
with, along with responses

00:12:22.577 --> 00:12:25.880
and scoring guides for each of those.

00:12:25.880 --> 00:12:29.000
So although that most of our questions

00:12:29.000 --> 00:12:30.680
in the AP courses on Khan Academy

00:12:30.680 --> 00:12:32.700
are multiple choice or
something close to it,

00:12:32.700 --> 00:12:33.890
they're all designed really

00:12:33.890 --> 00:12:37.354
with being able to answer free
response questions in mind.

00:12:37.354 --> 00:12:39.130
They take you through the thought process

00:12:39.130 --> 00:12:41.230
and the sort of modeling that thought

00:12:41.230 --> 00:12:42.889
that you would need in
order to be successful

00:12:42.889 --> 00:12:45.100
in the free response questions.

00:12:45.100 --> 00:12:46.342
So, even though you may be practicing

00:12:46.342 --> 00:12:48.310
and using the mastery system,

00:12:48.310 --> 00:12:50.546
using something multiple
choice or close to it,

00:12:50.546 --> 00:12:52.490
you're really laying that foundation

00:12:52.490 --> 00:12:54.838
to have a firm understanding
of all of the content

00:12:54.838 --> 00:12:57.200
that you need in order to be successful

00:12:57.200 --> 00:12:58.770
in those free response questions.

00:12:58.770 --> 00:13:01.000
So yeah, I would do mastery stuff first,

00:13:01.000 --> 00:13:02.618
then I would watch the Sal videos

00:13:02.618 --> 00:13:06.170
that go through the
free response questions

00:13:06.170 --> 00:13:07.420
from the past few years.

00:13:07.420 --> 00:13:08.710
And then finally at the end,

00:13:08.710 --> 00:13:11.460
I would look at those
CollegeBoard resources

00:13:11.460 --> 00:13:12.719
for free response questions

00:13:12.719 --> 00:13:14.900
and try to work through those on your own

00:13:14.900 --> 00:13:16.120
and look at those scoring guides

00:13:16.120 --> 00:13:17.310
and sample responses.

00:13:17.310 --> 00:13:19.106
And following that
progression over the next

00:13:19.106 --> 00:13:20.750
you know, four to six weeks

00:13:20.750 --> 00:13:21.900
or potentially a little bit longer

00:13:21.900 --> 00:13:23.837
depending on when your exam actually is,

00:13:23.837 --> 00:13:25.270
you should be in pretty good shape.

00:13:25.270 --> 00:13:27.260
There's a lot of resources
out there for everybody.

00:13:27.260 --> 00:13:28.980
Plus there's CollegeBoard resources

00:13:28.980 --> 00:13:29.991
like YouTube videos

00:13:29.991 --> 00:13:33.040
that they're doing live
YouTube review sessions

00:13:33.040 --> 00:13:34.720
for all of the different courses

00:13:34.720 --> 00:13:35.948
and you can get information for that

00:13:35.948 --> 00:13:38.676
both on our AP blog posts

00:13:38.676 --> 00:13:40.763
and also on the CollegeBoard website.

00:13:42.150 --> 00:13:43.930
- Super, super useful.

00:13:43.930 --> 00:13:45.780
And let's see, we have some questions.

00:13:45.780 --> 00:13:46.613
This is from YouTube.

00:13:46.613 --> 00:13:48.357
Pamela Criswell asks,

00:13:48.357 --> 00:13:50.304
what is your suggestion for the best ways

00:13:50.304 --> 00:13:51.892
for teachers to use Khan Academy

00:13:51.892 --> 00:13:55.420
since we have had to
go to remote learning?

00:13:55.420 --> 00:13:57.065
I could take a first stab of that Pamela

00:13:57.065 --> 00:14:00.298
and I'd love to hear what you
have to say as well, Adam.

00:14:00.298 --> 00:14:03.270
So I think there's several ways

00:14:03.270 --> 00:14:04.624
that you could use Khan Academy.

00:14:04.624 --> 00:14:06.811
Before the school closures,
when school was in session,

00:14:06.811 --> 00:14:08.630
what we've seen is

00:14:08.630 --> 00:14:11.626
if teachers are able
to have their students

00:14:11.626 --> 00:14:14.330
work on personalized learning,

00:14:14.330 --> 00:14:15.760
mastery learning on Khan Academy,

00:14:15.760 --> 00:14:17.160
even 45 minutes a week,

00:14:17.160 --> 00:14:19.040
that that has been associated

00:14:19.040 --> 00:14:20.500
with accelerated outcomes

00:14:20.500 --> 00:14:22.577
on a wide variety of measures.

00:14:22.577 --> 00:14:24.296
And that is something like,

00:14:24.296 --> 00:14:25.924
let's say you're a sixth grade teacher,

00:14:25.924 --> 00:14:28.645
you can assign the sixth
grade course mastery

00:14:28.645 --> 00:14:30.020
to your students

00:14:30.020 --> 00:14:31.620
and that's something
that could take all year.

00:14:31.620 --> 00:14:33.370
So could take a good amount of time,

00:14:33.370 --> 00:14:35.080
until the kids over the year

00:14:35.080 --> 00:14:36.610
are going to learn
their own time and pace,

00:14:36.610 --> 00:14:38.005
try to get things a leveled up

00:14:38.005 --> 00:14:39.260
and you as a teacher

00:14:39.260 --> 00:14:40.380
are going to get a dashboard

00:14:40.380 --> 00:14:41.820
where you can see who's progressing,

00:14:41.820 --> 00:14:43.482
who's not, who's engaged, who's not,

00:14:43.482 --> 00:14:45.500
who's getting stuck on certain concepts.

00:14:45.500 --> 00:14:46.730
And then in a class time,

00:14:46.730 --> 00:14:48.196
you can take some of
the kids who are stuck

00:14:48.196 --> 00:14:50.910
and you could do a focused
intervention with them.

00:14:50.910 --> 00:14:52.350
You can maybe pair them up

00:14:52.350 --> 00:14:53.530
or you could pair them with a student

00:14:53.530 --> 00:14:55.500
who might have already
mastered that concept.

00:14:55.500 --> 00:14:57.191
We've seen that work really well.

00:14:57.191 --> 00:15:00.280
Now that we have this
school closure situation,

00:15:00.280 --> 00:15:01.620
what we're seeing a lot of teachers do

00:15:01.620 --> 00:15:04.690
is lean on that a little bit more.

00:15:04.690 --> 00:15:07.820
Where they can now expect
instead of 45 minutes a week,

00:15:07.820 --> 00:15:08.920
depending on the age group,

00:15:08.920 --> 00:15:11.817
anywhere between 20 to 60 minutes a day

00:15:11.817 --> 00:15:14.003
of being able to work on their math

00:15:14.003 --> 00:15:15.620
at their own time and pace.

00:15:15.620 --> 00:15:18.210
One of the things we did a few weeks ago

00:15:18.210 --> 00:15:20.320
is we released these daily
schedule for students

00:15:20.320 --> 00:15:21.640
of different age groups.

00:15:21.640 --> 00:15:22.950
That articulated, hey,

00:15:22.950 --> 00:15:23.783
for this age group,

00:15:23.783 --> 00:15:26.440
maybe 45 minutes of Khan Academy math

00:15:26.440 --> 00:15:28.300
at this time of the day might make sense.

00:15:28.300 --> 00:15:29.460
What we're going to be releasing

00:15:29.460 --> 00:15:30.970
over the next couple of days

00:15:30.970 --> 00:15:32.700
are actually learning plans

00:15:32.700 --> 00:15:35.010
where not just over through
the end of the school year,

00:15:35.010 --> 00:15:37.277
but also potentially leveraging the summer

00:15:37.277 --> 00:15:40.510
so students can make sure
that they have a plan

00:15:40.510 --> 00:15:43.040
to cover all of the major concepts

00:15:43.040 --> 00:15:46.565
that they need to be at
grade level or above.

00:15:46.565 --> 00:15:49.010
For students who maybe are weak

00:15:49.010 --> 00:15:50.180
in some of their foundations,

00:15:50.180 --> 00:15:53.290
we recommend, let's say their
sixth grade students again,

00:15:53.290 --> 00:15:54.450
keep going on fifth grade,

00:15:54.450 --> 00:15:56.834
but simultaneously also try to get mastery

00:15:56.834 --> 00:15:58.850
on arithmetic on Khan Academy

00:15:58.850 --> 00:15:59.683
to make sure they have

00:15:59.683 --> 00:16:02.250
really, really, really strong foundations.

00:16:02.250 --> 00:16:03.570
Now as a teacher,

00:16:03.570 --> 00:16:05.842
one of the things I think we've
seen teachers do really well

00:16:05.842 --> 00:16:08.210
is if there's a certain time of day

00:16:08.210 --> 00:16:09.790
kids are working on Khan Academy

00:16:09.790 --> 00:16:10.750
at their own time and pace,

00:16:10.750 --> 00:16:11.640
you can monitor that.

00:16:11.640 --> 00:16:14.560
But also if you could
make yourself available

00:16:14.560 --> 00:16:18.016
via Google Hangouts or Zoom
or something like that,

00:16:18.016 --> 00:16:21.830
where students can jump on
maybe a couple of times a week,

00:16:21.830 --> 00:16:22.970
ask you questions,

00:16:22.970 --> 00:16:24.666
you can work through
work examples with them,

00:16:24.666 --> 00:16:27.190
and it really should feel like a tutorial

00:16:27.190 --> 00:16:28.620
because everyone's looking for

00:16:28.620 --> 00:16:29.840
that social connection right now.

00:16:29.840 --> 00:16:32.250
So the more interactive it is the better.

00:16:32.250 --> 00:16:33.550
So I think something like that,

00:16:33.550 --> 00:16:35.028
that type of flow is really powerful

00:16:35.028 --> 00:16:37.110
and also encouraging parents

00:16:37.110 --> 00:16:39.490
and students to continue
through the summer.

00:16:39.490 --> 00:16:41.168
You know we're in a tough
scenario right over here

00:16:41.168 --> 00:16:42.867
where it looks like most of the country,

00:16:42.867 --> 00:16:44.870
schools are going to be closed

00:16:44.870 --> 00:16:46.740
probably through the
end of the school year

00:16:46.740 --> 00:16:49.260
and so there's a loss there of two months,

00:16:49.260 --> 00:16:50.440
but then there's an opportunity

00:16:50.440 --> 00:16:52.050
to maybe leverage the summer as well

00:16:52.050 --> 00:16:54.700
to make sure students are at grade level.

00:16:54.700 --> 00:16:56.233
Adam, anything to add to that?

00:16:57.290 --> 00:17:00.063
- [Adam] No, I think you covered
that really, really well.

00:17:02.170 --> 00:17:03.662
- [Sal] All right.

00:17:03.662 --> 00:17:04.620
And I'll, if we think of more

00:17:04.620 --> 00:17:06.400
and all of you teachers out there,

00:17:06.400 --> 00:17:08.030
a lot of what we are trying
to recommend to teachers

00:17:08.030 --> 00:17:10.890
is coming from teachers doing a great job.

00:17:10.890 --> 00:17:13.630
So we're all learning this together.

00:17:13.630 --> 00:17:15.383
So send us your ideas as well.

00:17:16.260 --> 00:17:18.190
And so there is,

00:17:18.190 --> 00:17:19.350
so a quick question.

00:17:19.350 --> 00:17:21.940
So Camille B P (mumbles) says,

00:17:22.888 --> 00:17:24.460
do we have content for TOEFL?

00:17:24.460 --> 00:17:26.680
Unfortunately, we do
not have TOEFL content.

00:17:26.680 --> 00:17:28.997
That's the test for foreign students

00:17:28.997 --> 00:17:30.924
who need to show English proficiency.

00:17:30.924 --> 00:17:32.780
We don't have that yet,

00:17:32.780 --> 00:17:35.180
or we don't have plans for that.

00:17:35.180 --> 00:17:36.580
Let's see, we have a
question from YouTube.

00:17:36.580 --> 00:17:38.528
Susanna Garcia Dominguez says,

00:17:38.528 --> 00:17:40.740
how can I use your science course

00:17:40.740 --> 00:17:43.090
to prove that I have mastered
middle school science?

00:17:43.090 --> 00:17:46.353
So I have a few ideas, but Adam,
I'd love your take on that.

00:17:46.353 --> 00:17:47.234
- Yeah.

00:17:47.234 --> 00:17:48.250
That's a--

00:17:48.250 --> 00:17:50.710
We don't have specific content out

00:17:50.710 --> 00:17:53.150
for middle school classrooms yet.

00:17:53.150 --> 00:17:55.207
That's something that we're
hoping to work on in the future.

00:17:55.207 --> 00:17:57.700
But right now, I would
take a look at things

00:17:57.700 --> 00:17:59.012
like high school biology.

00:17:59.012 --> 00:18:01.996
We do have courses in
chemistry and in physics.

00:18:01.996 --> 00:18:05.143
And if you can work your
way through those courses,

00:18:05.143 --> 00:18:07.870
and we have all of those
resources on Khan Academy,

00:18:07.870 --> 00:18:09.430
that if you're a really motivated student,

00:18:09.430 --> 00:18:11.240
which it sounds like you really are,

00:18:11.240 --> 00:18:13.590
then you can watch those
videos, read articles,

00:18:13.590 --> 00:18:16.100
and you can continue to
practice those exercises

00:18:16.100 --> 00:18:18.175
over and over and see
new types of questions.

00:18:18.175 --> 00:18:20.610
And that's going to be a really great way

00:18:20.610 --> 00:18:24.598
for you to start to to build
that mastery in those courses.

00:18:24.598 --> 00:18:27.030
In terms of being able to
show that you've done that,

00:18:27.030 --> 00:18:29.576
I think that just having
that knowledge in your head

00:18:29.576 --> 00:18:32.070
and being able to talk with
that with your teachers

00:18:32.070 --> 00:18:34.060
and fellow students is the best way

00:18:34.060 --> 00:18:36.510
that we can help support that right now.

00:18:36.510 --> 00:18:38.633
But Sal what do you think?

00:18:38.633 --> 00:18:40.216
- Yeah, I would just add to that.

00:18:40.216 --> 00:18:42.740
What I've been telling a
lot of students is that,

00:18:42.740 --> 00:18:43.710
you know, some of these courses

00:18:43.710 --> 00:18:45.300
that are labeled high school courses,

00:18:45.300 --> 00:18:46.810
you could try engaging in.

00:18:46.810 --> 00:18:48.830
This is a great time to try it out.

00:18:48.830 --> 00:18:50.140
I think high school biology

00:18:50.140 --> 00:18:52.400
is something that a lot
of middle school students

00:18:52.400 --> 00:18:53.233
can engage in.

00:18:53.233 --> 00:18:54.660
You know, the math you need,

00:18:54.660 --> 00:18:55.730
you already have.

00:18:55.730 --> 00:18:57.880
And you know, you start
learning the basics.

00:18:57.880 --> 00:19:01.621
What is life? What is DNA?
RNA? Obviously what's a virus?

00:19:01.621 --> 00:19:04.180
Very, very relevant things right now.

00:19:04.180 --> 00:19:05.013
I think we're all a little bit

00:19:05.013 --> 00:19:06.160
more motivated than normal

00:19:06.160 --> 00:19:08.280
and to learn about
viruses, and RNA, and DNA,

00:19:08.280 --> 00:19:10.432
and things like that.

00:19:10.432 --> 00:19:13.190
And also on the math
side, exponential growth.

00:19:13.190 --> 00:19:15.370
So, I recommend high school biology

00:19:15.370 --> 00:19:16.620
is a great place to go.

00:19:16.620 --> 00:19:18.140
And if as a middle school student,

00:19:18.140 --> 00:19:19.130
you're able to engage in,

00:19:19.130 --> 00:19:20.478
it's going to be a great leg up,

00:19:20.478 --> 00:19:22.390
not just for high school and college.

00:19:22.390 --> 00:19:23.420
It's going to be a great.

00:19:23.420 --> 00:19:25.038
You'll sound smarter at
dinner parties these days.

00:19:25.038 --> 00:19:30.038
If you talk about how RNA is
more likely to mutate than NBA,

00:19:30.125 --> 00:19:31.670
you'll be able to keep up

00:19:31.670 --> 00:19:33.020
with some of the biologists out there.

00:19:33.020 --> 00:19:35.080
So that's what I would recommend.

00:19:35.080 --> 00:19:36.880
I'm actually even doing
it with some of my kids.

00:19:36.880 --> 00:19:38.610
Cause everyone's curious
right now about this.

00:19:38.610 --> 00:19:40.680
And I think you're ready to learn it,

00:19:40.680 --> 00:19:43.570
especially if you have
that motivation to do so.

00:19:43.570 --> 00:19:44.403
So let's see,

00:19:44.403 --> 00:19:49.010
we have from Facebook, Jane Molnar says,

00:19:49.010 --> 00:19:51.260
any plans for upper division math classes

00:19:51.260 --> 00:19:54.723
like abstract algebra, complex analysis?

00:19:55.750 --> 00:19:57.397
So I hear you,

00:19:57.397 --> 00:20:00.610
you know, as a math nerd,

00:20:00.610 --> 00:20:01.870
I'm talking to myself

00:20:01.870 --> 00:20:03.720
in the most affectionate possible way.

00:20:03.720 --> 00:20:05.700
I would love to do that at some point.

00:20:05.700 --> 00:20:08.260
Our focus has been
trying to do the courses

00:20:08.260 --> 00:20:11.120
that the most students have the
most trouble with right now.

00:20:11.120 --> 00:20:12.897
But I hope one day we do get into

00:20:12.897 --> 00:20:14.630
some of the more advanced.

00:20:14.630 --> 00:20:15.463
We do have things

00:20:15.463 --> 00:20:16.850
like multi-variable
differential equations,

00:20:16.850 --> 00:20:17.860
linear algebra,

00:20:17.860 --> 00:20:18.768
but you're really talking about,

00:20:18.768 --> 00:20:21.890
a complex analysis really is
around that level as well.

00:20:21.890 --> 00:20:23.810
So I hope we are able to get there

00:20:23.810 --> 00:20:25.160
over the next many years,

00:20:25.160 --> 00:20:28.063
but it's not part of
our pipeline just yet.

00:20:28.923 --> 00:20:31.540
Okay. So I have a Facebook,

00:20:31.540 --> 00:20:34.722
Adam (mumbles) to Sal and Adam.

00:20:34.722 --> 00:20:38.545
How will Khan Academy
get me into Stanford?

00:20:38.545 --> 00:20:40.702
(both laughing)

00:20:40.702 --> 00:20:43.890
Adam, I don't think
that's our job. (chuckles)

00:20:43.890 --> 00:20:46.203
- That's a not directly our job.

00:20:47.080 --> 00:20:49.030
- Not directly our job. (chuckles)

00:20:49.030 --> 00:20:50.670
What I will say,

00:20:50.670 --> 00:20:53.580
as I'm putting my
guidance counselor hat on,

00:20:53.580 --> 00:20:56.870
is first I wouldn't over
index on any one school.

00:20:56.870 --> 00:20:59.660
It's really more of what you make of it.

00:20:59.660 --> 00:21:01.600
But my advice generally,

00:21:01.600 --> 00:21:02.433
and it's great to have to say,

00:21:02.433 --> 00:21:03.660
hey, you know,

00:21:03.660 --> 00:21:05.210
this school or that school

00:21:05.210 --> 00:21:09.200
is where I could imagine
myself, is just be curious.

00:21:09.200 --> 00:21:10.780
Learn as much as you can.

00:21:10.780 --> 00:21:12.451
Do it for authentic reasons.

00:21:12.451 --> 00:21:15.740
Do other interesting things
above and beyond your academics,

00:21:15.740 --> 00:21:18.120
but don't do it just
to be able to fill out

00:21:18.120 --> 00:21:19.200
a college application.

00:21:19.200 --> 00:21:20.965
The college admissions folks that I know

00:21:20.965 --> 00:21:23.850
are good at spotting you know,

00:21:23.850 --> 00:21:25.150
engineered applications.

00:21:25.150 --> 00:21:26.660
They like authenticity.

00:21:26.660 --> 00:21:27.750
And you know,

00:21:27.750 --> 00:21:29.870
just to get to know
who you are as a person

00:21:29.870 --> 00:21:32.150
and how you might be able
to contribute to the world.

00:21:32.150 --> 00:21:34.280
And then when you apply to colleges,

00:21:34.280 --> 00:21:35.820
just be your authentic self.

00:21:35.820 --> 00:21:39.580
Don't try to be some
version of what you think

00:21:39.580 --> 00:21:42.020
that admissions officer wants to see.

00:21:42.020 --> 00:21:43.480
And if for whatever reason,

00:21:43.480 --> 00:21:45.997
your true authentic self
isn't what gets you in

00:21:45.997 --> 00:21:50.130
to some school, well that's probably good.

00:21:50.130 --> 00:21:51.440
It's like a marriage.

00:21:51.440 --> 00:21:53.760
(chuckles) If your true authentic self

00:21:53.760 --> 00:21:55.230
and the other person
doesn't want to marry you,

00:21:55.230 --> 00:21:57.422
well it's good to know
before you get married.

00:21:57.422 --> 00:22:00.030
Than after the fact. (laughing)

00:22:00.030 --> 00:22:02.011
So my advice is just be curious, learn,

00:22:02.011 --> 00:22:05.590
use this time right now to
work on interesting things.

00:22:05.590 --> 00:22:10.130
Ways to help your community,
inventions, or whatever else.

00:22:10.130 --> 00:22:11.750
You know, I've been thinking about it.

00:22:11.750 --> 00:22:13.410
Wouldn't it be cool if there are gloves,

00:22:13.410 --> 00:22:15.020
that not only protect you from the virus,

00:22:15.020 --> 00:22:17.210
but kill the virus on anything it touches.

00:22:17.210 --> 00:22:18.880
You know. I've been walking
into the supermarket

00:22:18.880 --> 00:22:21.670
with like Clorox wipes on both hands

00:22:21.670 --> 00:22:22.503
and I go to like the milk.

00:22:22.503 --> 00:22:23.336
I open it up. (chuckles)

00:22:23.336 --> 00:22:25.780
I'm like, not only am I protecting myself,

00:22:25.780 --> 00:22:28.170
but I'm protecting the next
person to open this door.

00:22:28.170 --> 00:22:31.120
So I think there's plenty of
opportunity for invention.

00:22:31.120 --> 00:22:35.806
Adam, any advice you have for Adam?

00:22:35.806 --> 00:22:37.570
- (laughing) Great name?

00:22:37.570 --> 00:22:39.595
Yeah, I think that the
that what you're saying

00:22:39.595 --> 00:22:41.055
rings really true.

00:22:41.055 --> 00:22:44.260
College admissions, counselors,
and all those folks,

00:22:44.260 --> 00:22:45.093
have seen it all before.

00:22:45.093 --> 00:22:47.760
They're gonna know if you're
just writing for that,

00:22:47.760 --> 00:22:49.150
you should really be writing for yourself.

00:22:49.150 --> 00:22:51.290
Be yourself and yeah,

00:22:51.290 --> 00:22:52.650
just to, as Sal said,

00:22:52.650 --> 00:22:54.223
be curious about the world around you

00:22:54.223 --> 00:22:56.848
and just be your authentic self,

00:22:56.848 --> 00:22:59.121
both before, during, and after

00:22:59.121 --> 00:23:01.282
the college admissions process.

00:23:01.282 --> 00:23:03.723
And things will generally work out.

00:23:05.360 --> 00:23:06.409
- Yeah.

00:23:06.409 --> 00:23:08.964
And we have this question from Instagram.

00:23:08.964 --> 00:23:11.533
Sarah_Moe66 says,

00:23:11.533 --> 00:23:12.964
what if students do not have

00:23:12.964 --> 00:23:15.993
reliable access to the internet.

00:23:17.190 --> 00:23:18.550
My advice is,

00:23:18.550 --> 00:23:19.430
you know,

00:23:19.430 --> 00:23:20.840
videos are a little bit harder

00:23:20.840 --> 00:23:23.520
with intermittent access to the internet.

00:23:23.520 --> 00:23:26.020
Our exercise platform
does leverage the internet

00:23:26.020 --> 00:23:27.850
to be able to keep track
of what you're doing.

00:23:27.850 --> 00:23:29.560
You're not, but it's not that intensive

00:23:29.560 --> 00:23:30.430
from a data point of view.

00:23:30.430 --> 00:23:32.740
So you should be able to
work on the exercises,

00:23:32.740 --> 00:23:34.630
get the feedback.

00:23:34.630 --> 00:23:37.660
And our Khan Academy's

00:23:37.660 --> 00:23:40.127
iPhone or tablet and phone app

00:23:40.127 --> 00:23:42.880
actually does allow you
to download and think,

00:23:42.880 --> 00:23:44.600
so you can do that kind
of thing overnight.

00:23:44.600 --> 00:23:45.670
And then you have a little bit

00:23:45.670 --> 00:23:47.910
more consistent of an experience.

00:23:47.910 --> 00:23:49.370
But this is a major problem.

00:23:49.370 --> 00:23:50.580
There's obviously a lot of kids,

00:23:50.580 --> 00:23:51.413
not just in the United States,

00:23:51.413 --> 00:23:53.800
but around the world, who
don't have access to internet.

00:23:53.800 --> 00:23:54.633
And we're going to,

00:23:54.633 --> 00:23:58.255
you know, we're not the folks
who can distribute laptops

00:23:58.255 --> 00:24:00.700
or distribute internet,

00:24:00.700 --> 00:24:02.760
but we are trying to work
with telecom carriers

00:24:02.760 --> 00:24:03.593
and other folks

00:24:03.593 --> 00:24:06.573
to see how we can help solve that issue.

00:24:07.620 --> 00:24:08.453
Let's see.

00:24:08.453 --> 00:24:13.453
Other questions that we are seeing.

00:24:13.460 --> 00:24:15.533
We are seeing.

00:24:16.770 --> 00:24:20.950
So YouTube Abel [Iiaudible] says,

00:24:20.950 --> 00:24:24.500
what can I do on Khan
Academy during this time?

00:24:24.500 --> 00:24:25.333
I don't know, Adam,

00:24:25.333 --> 00:24:26.166
do you want to take a first stab at that?

00:24:26.166 --> 00:24:27.420
There's a lot that you could do.

00:24:27.420 --> 00:24:28.980
- So many things.

00:24:28.980 --> 00:24:30.768
There are literally hundreds of courses

00:24:30.768 --> 00:24:33.478
on Khan Academy that you
can play around with.

00:24:33.478 --> 00:24:35.863
So I would suggest mixing
things up a little bit

00:24:35.863 --> 00:24:38.480
if you if you're in bio,

00:24:38.480 --> 00:24:40.030
if you're in high school biology course,

00:24:40.030 --> 00:24:42.374
or if you're, you know, in
fifth grade taking math,

00:24:42.374 --> 00:24:45.040
do a little bit of work in those courses.

00:24:45.040 --> 00:24:46.860
But also just sort of look around,

00:24:46.860 --> 00:24:48.320
poke around and follow your nose,

00:24:48.320 --> 00:24:51.113
follow your, whatever you
happen to be interested in.

00:24:51.113 --> 00:24:53.558
We have content on there from Pixar.

00:24:53.558 --> 00:24:55.270
Pixar in a box.

00:24:55.270 --> 00:24:56.990
If you're interested in animation.

00:24:56.990 --> 00:24:59.620
We have lots of computing
courses on there.

00:24:59.620 --> 00:25:01.180
So if you're interested
in computer science

00:25:01.180 --> 00:25:03.110
or learning something about programming,

00:25:03.110 --> 00:25:04.690
you can poke around in there.

00:25:04.690 --> 00:25:07.609
There's just a lot going on on
in the Khan Academy universe

00:25:07.609 --> 00:25:11.174
and I would just suggest
kinda opening things up

00:25:11.174 --> 00:25:12.950
and looking at something

00:25:12.950 --> 00:25:14.040
that maybe you haven't looked at before

00:25:14.040 --> 00:25:15.840
or something that you've
heard might be interesting

00:25:15.840 --> 00:25:17.070
and just play around.

00:25:17.070 --> 00:25:18.670
It's an open sandbox

00:25:18.670 --> 00:25:20.110
and I think that you'll find some,

00:25:20.110 --> 00:25:21.460
you'll find a lot of things in there

00:25:21.460 --> 00:25:23.700
that will be interesting to you,

00:25:23.700 --> 00:25:24.720
especially in this time,

00:25:24.720 --> 00:25:26.370
where you might have a
little bit more time,

00:25:26.370 --> 00:25:27.657
or a little bit more flexibility

00:25:27.657 --> 00:25:29.223
to get interested in things.

00:25:30.540 --> 00:25:32.815
- [Sal] And I'll just add
to what Adam said, Abel.

00:25:32.815 --> 00:25:34.420
Abell or Abel.

00:25:34.420 --> 00:25:37.920
You know, this bad situation

00:25:37.920 --> 00:25:39.071
that the world finds itself in it,

00:25:39.071 --> 00:25:42.854
there's a silver lining,
that you now have time.

00:25:42.854 --> 00:25:44.693
And if you can structure your day.

00:25:45.790 --> 00:25:47.498
You have every day you could spend,

00:25:47.498 --> 00:25:48.680
you know, sessions,

00:25:48.680 --> 00:25:49.560
I don't know how old you are,

00:25:49.560 --> 00:25:52.420
but you could spend sessions
of 20 minutes at a time,

00:25:52.420 --> 00:25:53.380
30 minutes at a time,

00:25:53.380 --> 00:25:54.820
maybe three or four of those sessions.

00:25:54.820 --> 00:25:58.664
You could make sure you have
super solid foundations in math

00:25:58.664 --> 00:26:00.750
by doing everything that
we've been talking about,

00:26:00.750 --> 00:26:01.790
not just in this live stream,

00:26:01.790 --> 00:26:02.710
but in previous ones,

00:26:02.710 --> 00:26:04.740
where you can get practice feedback,

00:26:04.740 --> 00:26:05.703
take that course challenge.

00:26:05.703 --> 00:26:08.440
If you're feeling a little
bit weak in these subjects,

00:26:08.440 --> 00:26:12.030
you can remediate with things
like our arithmetic course

00:26:12.030 --> 00:26:13.490
or a pre algebra course.

00:26:13.490 --> 00:26:14.690
And if you're feeling good,

00:26:14.690 --> 00:26:16.846
just keep going between now
and the start of school.

00:26:16.846 --> 00:26:21.610
I can't tell you enough how
confident that will make you

00:26:21.610 --> 00:26:23.770
in whatever subject you you engage in.

00:26:23.770 --> 00:26:24.813
So that can be in math.

00:26:24.813 --> 00:26:26.870
I encourage folks who feel they're ready,

00:26:26.870 --> 00:26:28.030
even at the middle school level,

00:26:28.030 --> 00:26:29.050
especially at the high school level,

00:26:29.050 --> 00:26:31.720
get a leg up on biology,
chemistry and physics.

00:26:31.720 --> 00:26:33.195
Because you have the time now,

00:26:33.195 --> 00:26:34.599
but then a year or two from now

00:26:34.599 --> 00:26:36.050
when you're taking those courses

00:26:36.050 --> 00:26:37.217
and you're taking six or seven courses

00:26:37.217 --> 00:26:38.050
and you have homework,

00:26:38.050 --> 00:26:40.359
you're like, oh I wish
I used those five months

00:26:40.359 --> 00:26:41.459
a little bit better.

00:26:41.459 --> 00:26:44.025
I would also encourage
you read a lot right now

00:26:44.025 --> 00:26:46.332
and think critically about that reading.

00:26:46.332 --> 00:26:48.430
I would, even if you're not planning

00:26:48.430 --> 00:26:49.755
on taking the SAT in
the next several months,

00:26:49.755 --> 00:26:53.110
I actually think the SAT
practice on Khan Academy

00:26:53.110 --> 00:26:54.460
that we've been doing
with the CollegeBoard,

00:26:54.460 --> 00:26:56.653
it's not just in math, it's in
reading and writing as well.

00:26:56.653 --> 00:26:58.588
It's just great practice in general

00:26:58.588 --> 00:26:59.963
to sharpen those skills

00:26:59.963 --> 00:27:01.470
and get you ready for college.

00:27:01.470 --> 00:27:02.730
It's related to this question

00:27:02.730 --> 00:27:05.172
from YouTube, Sri Harsha (mumbles) says,

00:27:05.172 --> 00:27:10.172
do you guys have plans
to do a guide for the GRE

00:27:10.190 --> 00:27:11.340
as y'all have done for the SAT?

00:27:11.340 --> 00:27:14.440
And Sri Harsha is very generous.

00:27:14.440 --> 00:27:16.640
You guys really, really
helped me out on this

00:27:16.640 --> 00:27:17.960
and I can't thank you guys enough.

00:27:17.960 --> 00:27:19.650
Well thank you Sri Harsha.

00:27:19.650 --> 00:27:22.300
Unfortunately, we don't
have GRE prep right now.

00:27:22.300 --> 00:27:23.133
But you know,

00:27:23.133 --> 00:27:26.060
I think that SAT prep is
fairly general purpose.

00:27:26.060 --> 00:27:28.059
A lot of folks have also
asked about the ACT.

00:27:28.059 --> 00:27:30.650
Alison Gabble on Facebook asked about it.

00:27:30.650 --> 00:27:32.589
We don't have ACT specific prep either,

00:27:32.589 --> 00:27:34.730
but once again, if you get really good,

00:27:34.730 --> 00:27:36.061
these things are all
highly, highly correlated.

00:27:36.061 --> 00:27:37.228
SAT, GRE, ACT.

00:27:38.244 --> 00:27:41.910
If you use our SAT work and
get really strong at it,

00:27:41.910 --> 00:27:46.560
that's going to transfer
I believe to other tests.

00:27:46.560 --> 00:27:49.280
So it looks like we're all at time,

00:27:49.280 --> 00:27:50.113
it's always fun.

00:27:50.113 --> 00:27:52.490
I want to thank Adam for joining us.

00:27:52.490 --> 00:27:53.842
He does, you know,

00:27:53.842 --> 00:27:56.930
a lot of folks think that Khan Academy

00:27:56.930 --> 00:27:58.830
is still a walk in closet,

00:27:58.830 --> 00:28:00.020
although I am backing up.

00:28:00.020 --> 00:28:04.560
I'm in my, in our kids kind
of a work room right now.

00:28:04.560 --> 00:28:06.470
But it's a team effort.

00:28:06.470 --> 00:28:08.760
And what you see as a finished product

00:28:08.760 --> 00:28:11.523
of over 200 folks, Adam
leads a large chunk of that.

00:28:11.523 --> 00:28:14.570
And you know, folks hopefully appreciate

00:28:14.570 --> 00:28:15.810
that there's a lot of folks

00:28:15.810 --> 00:28:18.280
who are dedicating their time
and energy to this effort.

00:28:18.280 --> 00:28:21.140
And I want to thank all of them.

00:28:21.140 --> 00:28:23.150
And you know, before we head out,

00:28:23.150 --> 00:28:24.330
I just want to remind everyone,

00:28:24.330 --> 00:28:27.380
you know, stay safe, stay
healthy, stay socially distant.

00:28:27.380 --> 00:28:29.640
That's the best way that
we can stop the spread

00:28:29.640 --> 00:28:31.680
of what we're all going through.

00:28:31.680 --> 00:28:33.770
And I want to thank you for joining

00:28:33.770 --> 00:28:35.140
because not only is this a way

00:28:35.140 --> 00:28:36.193
for all of you to feel connected,

00:28:36.193 --> 00:28:38.470
it's a way for us to
feel connected to you.

00:28:38.470 --> 00:28:40.220
Which we all need right now.

00:28:40.220 --> 00:28:41.370
And I'll just say again,

00:28:41.370 --> 00:28:43.708
a reminder Khan Academy
is a not for profit.

00:28:43.708 --> 00:28:46.430
We are dependent on
philanthropic donations

00:28:46.430 --> 00:28:47.830
from folks like yourself.

00:28:47.830 --> 00:28:49.210
Every dollar matters.

00:28:49.210 --> 00:28:50.937
So if you're in a position to do so,

00:28:50.937 --> 00:28:53.280
please think about
donating to Khan Academy.

00:28:53.280 --> 00:28:55.950
You can see the links at the end.

00:28:55.950 --> 00:28:57.716
It's khanacademy.org/donate.

00:28:57.716 --> 00:28:59.110
Thank you so much.

00:28:59.110 --> 00:29:00.483
Have a great Monday.

